{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.LadderFrameChange","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.LadderFrameChange","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.Field.Basic","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.FieldSimp","lean:module:Mathlib.Tactic.Ring"],"declarations":4,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.u_rung_order_is_even","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.u_rung_order_is_even","kind":"theorem","line":92,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/LadderFrameChange.lean#L92-L98","doc":"**An odd `h`-order cannot be produced by a `u`-rung.**\n\nContrapositive form of the above, stated so it can be cited directly: if a\n`u`-rung of order `k` is written as a multiple of `hᵐ`, then `m` is even.\nNothing about `b` or `q` enters — it is forced by the exponent arithmetic. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.u_pow_eq_even_h_pow","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.u_pow_eq_even_h_pow","kind":"theorem","line":76,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/LadderFrameChange.lean#L76-L90","doc":"**Every `u`-rung is an even `h`-rung: `uᵏ = (b/(q+1)²)ᵏ · h²ᵏ`.**\n\nA `u`-coefficient at order `k` contributes at `h`-order exactly `2k`. So the\n`u`-frame ladder places weight only on **even** `h`-orders, and odd ones are\nunreachable — not unpopulated by choice, but outside the span of any\n`u`-ladder whatsoever.\n\nThis is the exact statement behind \"the `u¹` rung is an artifact of expanding\nin a squared variable\", and behind the half-integer powers seen when the\n`u`-expansion is pushed past its analytic range. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.u_eq_b_mul_h_sq_div","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.u_eq_b_mul_h_sq_div","kind":"theorem","line":65,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/LadderFrameChange.lean#L65-L74","doc":"**`u = b·h² / (q+1)²`** — the exact frame-change identity.\n\n`u ∼ h²` is not an approximation near `q = 1`; it holds at every `q`, with\nconversion factor `b/(q+1)²`. At the proton anchor that factor is ≈ 0.1949,\nwhich is why `u/h²` measures 0.194875 rather than the `√3/8 ≈ 0.2165` its\n`q → 1` limit would suggest. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.hbar_mul_succ_eq_h","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.hbar_mul_succ_eq_h","kind":"theorem","line":55,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/LadderFrameChange.lean#L55-L63","doc":"**`ℏ_q · (q+1) = h`.**\n\nThe substrate's two first-order quantities differ by exactly the factor\n`q + 1`: `ℏ_q = (q−1)/q` and `h = q − 1/q = (q²−1)/q`. This is what makes the\nframe change exact rather than asymptotic. ","signatureChunk":0}]}